go`, `static/canopy.*`, `templates/*` differ because HEAD (0b497c8) added the drawer.\n\nLet me see the branch's own commit diff to verify what it touches:\n- `git show 0b497c8 --stat`\n\nThen merge origin/master into the branch. Since the changes are in different files (probably), a merge should be clean. Let me check.\n\nAlso the verdict said \"Required state: the branch tip must contain current origin/master.\" A merge accomplishes that. Alternatively a rebase. For forest-style workflows with the \"approval Gate\" that checks `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master <revision>`, either works.\n\nWhich approach is more appropriate? The Verifier said \"Required state: the branch tip must contain current origin/master.\" Both merge and rebase satisfy that. Rebases rewrite history; the rejected revision is the branch tip 0b497c8. The Verifier's check `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master <sha>` will pass either way.\n\nI think rebasing onto origin/master is cleaner (fast-forward base, single feature commit on top). But a merge commit is also fine and less invasive. Let me check what the branch commit touches first.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Interesting. There's a bigger diff between HEAD and origin/master than just the two commits. Wait, `git diff --stat HEAD origin/master` shows a full forward diff, which includes:\\n- The changes in the branch (0b497c8 slide-over log drawer) that aren't on master — those appear as deletions in reverse... actually diff HEAD..origin/master shows what master has that HEAD doesn't, but since the diff is symmetric (two-way), it shows all differences including both directions. The stat shows combined changes.\\n\\nActually `git diff HEAD origin/master` shows the diff to transform HEAD into origin/master. So it includes:\\n- Reverting the slide-over drawer changes from HEAD (in reverse: static/canopy.js -148, templates/log.html -11 in master vs HEAD? hmm)\\n- Adding the ca9e237 discovery changes and f2c0e7b agent scoping.\\n\\nThe important part: the branch must fast-forward to current origin/master, i.e., the branch tip must contain origin/master as an ancestor. The proper repair: merge origin/master into the branch (or rebase). Since the verdict requires \\\"the branch tip must contain current origin/master\\\" — the branch must fast-forward from origin/master. Given the approval Gate requires \\\"Revision fast-forwards origin/master\\\", the repair should make the new revision have origin/master as an ancestor.\\n\\nThe cleanest approach: rebase the branch's commit onto origin/master, or merge origin/master into the branch tip. Let me check for conflicts. The branch commit (0b497c8) touches slide-over log drawer files: presumably static/canopy.js, static/canopy.css, templates/log.html, server_test.go, etc. The master commits ca9e237 touches discovery.go, discovery_test.go and f2c0e7b touches agents/critic, agents/tester files.\\n\\nWait — the diff stat shows `discovery.go` and `discovery_test.go` differ between HEAD and master (from ca9e237), `agents/...` differ (from f2c0e7b), and also `server_test.go`, `static/canopy.*`, `templates/*` differ because HEAD (0b497c8) added the drawer.\\n\\nLet me see the branch's own commit diff to verify what it touches:\\n- `git show 0b497c8 --stat`\\n\\nThen merge origin/master into the branch. Since the changes are in different files (probably), a merge should be clean. Let me check.\\n\\nAlso the verdict said \\\"Required state: the branch tip must contain current origin/master.\\\" A merge accomplishes that. Alternatively a rebase. For forest-style workflows with the \\\"approval Gate\\\" that checks `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master <revision>`, either works.\\n\\nWhich approach is more appropriate? The Verifier said \\\"Required state: the branch tip must contain current origin/master.\\\" Both merge and rebase satisfy that. Rebases rewrite history; the rejected revision is the branch tip 0b497c8. The Verifier's check `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master <sha>` will pass either way.\\n\\nI think rebasing onto origin/master is cleaner (fast-forward base, single feature commit on top). But a merge commit is also fine and less invasive. Let me check what the branch commit touches first.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me inspect what the branch commit itself changed and check how `origin/master`'s two new commits interact."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_51db628b1030405cba79ab19","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788549208185309919-fixer && git show 0b497c8 --stat | head -30 && echo \"--- master commits ---\" && git show ca9e237 --stat | head -15 && echo \"---\" && git show f2c0e7b --stat | head -15"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731","usage":{"input":553,"output":843,"cacheRead":15104,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":762,"totalTokens":16500,"cost":{"input":0.00002765,"output":0.0000843,"cacheRead":0.00015104,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00026299}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788549239566,"responseId":"gen-1788549239-DXIysQfu8L8Zq0Fqq6na","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_51db628b1030405cba79ab19","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"commit 0b497c89d546078d12cb0612bcf37bdd6f39763c\nAuthor: Iron Forest Builder <builder@forest.invalid>\nDate:   Fri Sep 4 13:51:54 2026 -0500\n\n    feat(canopy): open run logs in an accessible slide-over drawer\n\n server_test.go          |  47 ++++++++++++++-\n static/canopy.css       |  98 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-\n static/canopy.js        | 148 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n templates/instance.html |   4 +-\n templates/log.html      |  11 ++--\n templates/page.html     |  11 ++++\n 6 files changed, 308 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)\n--- master commits ---\ncommit ca9e23